1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,675
'Banana Money': The Wartime
Currency That Inflated to Nothing

2
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:08,889
A $10 note from the Japanese
Occupation, its face printed

3
00:00:08,969 --> 00:00:12,958
with a bunch of bananas,
sells today to collectors for

4
00:00:13,038 --> 00:00:16,348
a few dollars — which
is, almost to the cent,

5
00:00:16,428 --> 00:00:21,325
what it was actually worth by the
time Japan surrendered in 1945.

6
00:00:21,325 --> 00:00:24,292
The note promises "THE JAPANESE GOVERNMENT

7
00:00:24,372 --> 00:00:28,000
PROMISES TO PAY THE BEARER
ON DEMAND TEN DOLLARS."

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,850
It never did.

9
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:33,413
Within three and a half years,
it went from being worth

10
00:00:33,493 --> 00:00:36,725
exactly as much as the
currency it replaced to not

11
00:00:36,805 --> 00:00:40,250
being worth the paper it
was printed on — literally.

12
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:43,913
After Singapore fell on
the 15th of February 1942,

13
00:00:43,993 --> 00:00:49,828
the Japanese military administration
introduced its own currency to replace the

14
00:00:49,908 --> 00:00:54,550
Straits and Malayan dollars, at
first pegged at par with them.

15
00:00:54,550 --> 00:01:00,809
The earliest notes — cents through $10 —
carried the usual anti-counterfeiting features:

16
00:01:00,889 --> 00:01:04,275
serial numbers, a watermark,
a security thread.

17
00:01:04,275 --> 00:01:09,091
The $10 note's design, a bunch
of bananas under a banana tree,

18
00:01:09,171 --> 00:01:12,725
gave the whole currency
its lasting nickname.

19
00:01:12,725 --> 00:01:16,463
Malays had their own name
for it, reportedly ruder than

20
00:01:16,543 --> 00:01:20,500
the English one and unprintable
in the papers of the day.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,700
The problem was never the design.

22
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:27,717
It was that nothing backed the
currency except Japan's word,

23
00:01:27,797 --> 00:01:32,633
and Japan printed more of it every
time it needed money — to pay troops,

24
00:01:32,713 --> 00:01:37,425
to pay for requisitioned goods, to
fund a war it was steadily losing.

25
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:41,000
There was no gold, no silver, no limit.

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,436
As the printing accelerated, the
security features were the first

27
00:01:45,516 --> 00:01:49,465
thing to go: later notes carried
no serial numbers at all,

28
00:01:49,545 --> 00:01:54,200
and counterfeiting became rampant
on top of the official inflation.

29
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:59,211
New, larger denominations
followed the money down —

30
00:01:59,291 --> 00:02:03,504
a $100 note by 1944,
a $1,000 note by 1945,

31
00:02:03,584 --> 00:02:08,675
for a currency that had
started out topping at $10.

32
00:02:08,675 --> 00:02:13,225
The results were exactly what
that combination produces.

33
00:02:13,225 --> 00:02:16,595
An egg that cost 3 cents before the war

34
00:02:16,675 --> 00:02:20,125
cost $100 by the end of the occupation.

35
00:02:20,125 --> 00:02:22,755
A hundred catties of rice —

36
00:02:22,835 --> 00:02:27,250
about 60 kilograms —
went from $5 to $5,000.

37
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:32,251
By August 1945, the Japanese
administration had issued something

38
00:02:32,331 --> 00:02:35,586
like $4,000 million worth of the currency,

39
00:02:35,666 --> 00:02:41,937
against a normal pre-war circulation for
the whole region of about $220 million:

40
00:02:42,017 --> 00:02:46,225
nearly twenty times the
money chasing the same goods.

41
00:02:46,225 --> 00:02:50,172
Then, on the 8th of September
1945, less than a month

42
00:02:50,252 --> 00:02:53,592
after Japan's surrender,
the British Military

43
00:02:53,672 --> 00:02:57,923
Administration announced what
everyone already suspected:

44
00:02:58,003 --> 00:03:01,650
banana money would be
given no value whatsoever.

45
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:05,017
There was no exchange
rate to negotiate down to,

46
00:03:05,097 --> 00:03:09,550
no gradual write-off — the
currency simply ceased to be money.

47
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:12,596
Anyone who had quietly
kept pre-war Straits or

48
00:03:12,676 --> 00:03:15,451
Malayan dollars through the occupation had

49
00:03:15,531 --> 00:03:18,725
those honoured the moment
the British returned.

50
00:03:18,725 --> 00:03:21,983
Anyone whose entire savings existed only

51
00:03:22,063 --> 00:03:25,150
in banana money woke up with nothing.

52
00:03:25,150 --> 00:03:30,275
Japan has never compensated holders
of the notes, then or since.

53
00:03:30,275 --> 00:03:35,575
What happened to the actual paper
afterwards is its own small irony.

54
00:03:35,575 --> 00:03:39,649
Banks and buildings across
the city were still sitting on

55
00:03:39,729 --> 00:03:43,219
vaults and basements full
of it — by one account,

56
00:03:43,299 --> 00:03:48,400
more than 400 tons of it, some 20,000
cases, found in Singapore alone.

57
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:51,607
Rather than let it
circulate as a curiosity,

58
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:54,900
the British had it
repulped into newsprint.

59
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:58,937
The same notes that, months
earlier, hadn't been enough to buy

60
00:03:59,017 --> 00:04:03,200
an egg became the paper people
read the next morning's news on.

61
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:08,099
The demonetisation also left a legal
mess that took years to sort out:

62
00:04:08,179 --> 00:04:12,651
whether debts paid in banana money
during the occupation counted

63
00:04:12,731 --> 00:04:16,065
as paid at all became a
real courtroom question,

64
00:04:16,145 --> 00:04:21,755
still being argued as a High Court test
case in 1949 and settled by a Debtor and

65
00:04:21,835 --> 00:04:26,600
Creditor (Occupation) ordinance
only toward the end of that decade.

66
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,475
Why it matters today: the
collectors who now pay a few

67
00:04:30,555 --> 00:04:33,625
dollars for a "banana"
ten-dollar note are,

68
00:04:33,705 --> 00:04:38,100
in a strange way, finally paying
it its 1945 exchange value.

69
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:41,794
The currency was never really
money in the way the Straits

70
00:04:41,874 --> 00:04:46,608
dollar it replaced was money — it was
a wartime promise printed on demand,

71
00:04:46,688 --> 00:04:49,861
worth exactly as much as
the government behind it,

72
00:04:49,941 --> 00:04:53,650
and once that government
surrendered, so did the promise.
